  <section class=3D"block">
    <h2><span class=3D"num">03</span> What "code graph" actually means here=
</h2>
    <p>Two different graphs are doing work in this design, and it's worth k=
eeping them straight =E2=80=94 you'll get asked to distinguish them:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The code knowledge graph (data structure):</strong> parse=
 each file's AST (Roslyn for C#, or tree-sitter if the stack is mixed), and=
 extract nodes for functions/methods and referenced config keys (<code>send=
ToSfdc</code>, <code>plCtaControlMaster</code>, feature flags), with edges =
for <code>calls</code>, <code>reads_config</code>, and <code>imports</code>=
. This replaces the flat <code>git_index_raw</code> Delta table with someth=
ing that has structure, not just rows.</li>
      <li><strong>The agent orchestration graph (LangGraph):</strong> a sta=
teful ReAct loop with graph-aware tools =E2=80=94 <code>resolve_entry_point=
</code>, <code>get_function</code>, <code>get_callers</code>, <code>get_con=
fig_value</code>, <code>trace_data_flow</code> =E2=80=94 so the agent decid=
es which node to pull next based on what it just read, instead of a fixed 2=
-step chain.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class=3D"callout"><strong>Why this matters for the interview:</str=
ong> it's the same principle you used twice already =E2=80=94 at AIA (Super=
visor =E2=86=92 Deep Agent, specialize instead of one do-everything agent) =
and in the Bajaj ops agent (deterministic lookup before LLM reasoning, not =
instead of it). This extends that same discipline one level deeper: give th=
e model a way to <em>navigate</em> structure instead of a way to <em>guess<=
/em> across it.</div>
  </section>

